<Guide slug="mac-tools-that-send-screenshots-to-a-remote-agent">
  <p class="lede">
    When your coding agent runs on a server, a screenshot on your Mac has to travel there before
    the agent can see it. At least eleven tools do that job today. They differ in how you trigger
    them, what they put on the server and what they cost. Hostbeam is ours; it is here like the
    rest, checked the same way.
  </p>

  <section>
    <h2>Three questions decide it</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Only screenshots from your Mac, or files too, and from your iPhone?</b> Most of these
        tools send images from a Mac; a few take any file; one sends from a phone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Do you also need to see what the agent builds?</b> When it starts a dev server on the
        server, two of these open it on your Mac.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>One app, or any app?</b> Some work inside a single terminal or editor; others work with
        whatever terminal and agent you use.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The four ways they work</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <b>Watch the clipboard.</b> Every image you copy is uploaded, and the clipboard is swapped
        for its path on the server. Nothing to press. Paste2SSH, SSH Screenshot Bridge.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>You send it.</b> A shortcut, a command or a click. Nothing leaves until you ask.
        Hostbeam, screencp, clipssh, SSH Image Drop, Image Paste for Remote SSH,
        claude-screenshot-uploader.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Catch the paste in your terminal.</b> You paste as usual and the tool uploads on the way.
        FainLane, and cmux for files you drop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <b>Tunnel the clipboard to the server.</b> Ctrl+V on the server reaches back to your Mac.
        clipaste.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Whichever it is, the agent ends up with a path. Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI and OpenCode
      attach the image when you give them its path (<a
        href="/guides/paste-a-screenshot-into-claude-code-over-ssh/">why</a
      >).
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>At a glance</h2>
