    <p class="aside">
      — means the tool's own pages don't offer it, as of the date above. The table leaves out how
      each one sends: Paste2SSH and SSH Screenshot Bridge, for one, upload every image you copy with
      nothing to press. The next section credits what each does best.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Each one, fairly</h2>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://paste2ssh.com/">Paste2SSH</a></h3>
    <p>
      Turn on Paste Mode and every image you copy is uploaded, with the clipboard swapped for the
      remote path; you can also drop files on its window, several at once. It uses your keys and
      <code class="mono">~/.ssh/config</code>, switches hosts in a click, keeps your last 100 paths
      and holds the connection open so later uploads are quick. Free with an optional tip, macOS 14
      and later, not open source. A Linux build is in alpha. Last release June 2026.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/stefanomainardi/ssh-screenshot-bridge">SSH Screenshot Bridge</a></h3>
    <p>
      Watches the clipboard and uploads with scp to hosts from your SSH config or your Tailscale
      network, and deletes the previous upload so only one screenshot sits on the server. Images
      only. macOS 15 and later, built from source.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/puma314/screencp">screencp</a></h3>
    <p>
      Click the menu-bar icon, pick a screenshot from a grid, pick a host, press Send. It copies the
      path, <code class="mono">host:path</code> or the whole scp command, as you prefer. Images
      only. macOS 14 and later, unsigned.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/mdrzn/claude-screenshot-uploader">claude-screenshot-uploader</a></h3>
    <p>
      An xbar plugin that watches your screenshots folder, or uploads on a click, with rsync over
      SSH to servers you list in it, then copies the remote path. Keeps an activity log. PNG and
      JPEG, MIT.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/samuellawrentz/clipssh">clipssh</a></h3>
    <p>
      A Bash script: copy a screenshot, run <code class="mono">clipssh myserver</code>, and the path
      is on your clipboard. Follows your SSH config, works from a Mac or Linux desktop. PNG, MIT.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://raycast.com/wjsong/ssh-image-drop">SSH Image Drop</a></h3>
    <p>
      A Raycast extension: a command, or a hotkey per server, sends the clipboard image or the files
      selected in Finder, several at once, and can pull files back. It signs in with a password kept
      in the Keychain or a key it installs. MIT.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=asfeng.claude-code-image-paste">Image Paste for Remote SSH</a></h3>
    <p>
      For Claude Code in a VS Code Remote-SSH terminal: Alt+I saves the clipboard image on the
      server and types its path. Nothing else to set up if you already work in VS Code. PNG, MIT.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://fainlane.com/">FainLane</a></h3>
    <p>
      Paste with Ctrl+V as usual in Ghostty, iTerm2 or Terminal: FainLane uploads the image, types
      the path at your cursor and finds the host from the session you're in, tmux and mosh
      included. Images only. Needs Accessibility and Automation permissions. Apple silicon, macOS
      15.7 and later; $12 a year after a 14-day trial.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://cmux.com/docs/ssh">cmux</a></h3>
    <p>
      A full terminal app for coding agents: drop an image or any file into a remote session and
      it's uploaded over that session's connection. Its browser panes reach the server's network,
      so <code class="mono">localhost:3000</code> in cmux is the remote dev server. It installs a
      small helper on the server the first time you connect, and also does mosh. Free and open
      source.
    </p>

    <h3><a rel="nofollow" href="https://github.com/hqhq1025/clipaste">clipaste</a></h3>
    <p>
      A daemon on your Mac serves the clipboard image through an SSH tunnel, and a one-time setup
      puts a stand-in for <code class="mono">xclip</code> on the server, so Ctrl+V in Claude Code
      on a Linux server just works; Codex runs a helper that prints a path. It needs the server to
      allow SSH port forwarding, which hardened servers often turn off. Also runs on Windows and
      Linux desktops. MIT.
    </p>

    <h3>Hostbeam</h3>
    <p>
      Copy a screenshot, or a file in Finder, and press <code class="mono">⌘⇧7</code>, or drop it on
      the menu-bar icon: it's uploaded to the host you picked and its path is on your clipboard.
      Any file up to 100 MB, several at once. It checks the host key before anything is sent, never
      overwrites a file, and installs nothing on the server.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        IPHONE_APP_LIVE ? (
          <li>
            A free <a href={APP_STORE_URL}>iPhone app</a> sends from the share sheet, a widget or
            Back Tap.
          </li>
        ) : (
          <li>An iPhone app sends from the share sheet, once it's on the App Store.</li>
        )
      }
      <li>
        It also <a href="/guides/open-a-remote-dev-server-on-your-mac/">opens the dev server</a> your
        agent started, in whichever browser you use, even where SSH port forwarding is off.
      </li>
      <li>
        Its free <a href="/agents/">MCP server</a> lets the agent fetch your latest screenshot
        itself.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Free with no limit. After the first week it asks now and then for a $29 licence, and never
      blocks anything. macOS 13 and later.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Which one</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>If you want</th><th>Look at</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>the phone too, the dev server back, any file, with any terminal and agent</td><td>Hostbeam</td></tr>
          <tr><td>one app for the terminal and the browser</td><td>cmux</td></tr>
          <tr><td>nothing to press at all, Mac screenshots only</td><td>Paste2SSH</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Ctrl+V to just work in your terminal</td><td>FainLane, or clipaste if forwarding is allowed</td></tr>
          <tr><td>to stay in Raycast or VS Code</td><td>SSH Image Drop, the VS Code extension</td></tr>
          <tr><td>a script you can read</td><td>clipssh</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      None of the others says it sends from an iPhone; on a phone, Termius uploads an image you
      paste into a session. If you make one of these tools and something here is wrong or out of
      date, write to <a href="mailto:support@hostbeam.app">support@hostbeam.app</a> and it gets
      fixed.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
